{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/804694-lancea-et-sanctum-and-theology","id":804694,"title":"Lancea et Sanctum and Theology","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-01-09T14:17:36-08:00","id":804694,"title":"Lancea et Sanctum and Theology","content":"So while I am not religious, the church my sister got married in over Christmas is running a series of lectures on Friday night entitled &quot;Theology for Non-Theologians.&quot; Since much of my history degree has a basis in the history of religion, I thought it would be an interesting thing to attend for a change of pace. It was stimulating, and gave me some stuff to think about. It also, totally unintentionally turned out to be pretty inspiring for Chronicles of Darkness. <br /> <br /> A nice chunk of ideas that I got from the lecture was about how the vampires in Chronicles of Darkness might relate to Christian Theology, particularly through the lens of their interactions with other covenants. <br /> <br /> Since the lecture was about &quot;Theology for non-Theologians,&quot; we started by defining what a Theologian actually was. Our lectures take on his profession was almost like it was a peer review process for faith and religious practices. It is the job of the theologian to take people's beliefs and test them in practice. The theologian checks that the experience of these practices has a positive impact upon the faithful, that they are consistent with the texts and teachings of the church, and that they are also internally coherent. The fusion of rigorous testing with profound faith bring to mind for me the relationship between the Lancea et Sanctum and the Ordo Dracul. <br /> <br /> These too covenants usually stand pretty far apart, since the Lancea sees the vampiric condition as being divinely bestowed (in a damning way, yes, but still divine), while the Ordo wants to transcend that very same condition. The conflict is obvious, but does it have to be inevitable? In most cases they will clash, but I think I see a way that they could synthesize their beliefs together, if they are working with the definition of a theologian given above. <br /> <br /> The Lancea's purpose is to be God's monsters, to test the living in their faith, and to protect the truly faithful. That's not a far cry from the Ordo's practice of testing an experimenting with the vampire's condition to overcome its weakness. So where would they find their common ground? <br /> <br /> Building a better monster for God. <br /> <br /> The Lancea is all about action. Their sanctification cannot come through faith alone, it must come through acting upon God's will. The exulted are those that serve God, not those that beg for scraps but do nothing. Couldn't it follow that if God requires the vampires to act to test the mortal flock, they could not also refine themselves to make themselves better servants? Clearly in LetS's point of view, God wants zhers servants to take the initiative. Zhe gave them some of the tools they needed in zhers divine curse, but they need to do the rest. Couldn't that mean refining the curse and becoming a more efficient monster? I suspect that many of the old school or conservative vampiric crowds would reject that logic, but there could be enough to at least consider it as a PC/NPC idea.<br /> <br /> (As an aside, I think that a similar line of logic would be cool for a Null Mysteriis Hunter, a theologian who is keen on combining the scientific method with their religious practice. As soon as they discover that the supernatural does exists and that scientific observations can be made about them, that redoubles their efforts to grow and evolve their faith through observation, reflection, and experimentation.) <br /> <br /> The other big idea that I pulled out for Vampire (although it really applies to all of CofD) cam from the question period at the end. Someone asked what commonality the lecturer saw in the students of theology that he teaches, since it is neither as prestigious as it used to be, nor is it a profession that can be more the part-time. His answer was super interesting. All of them seem to be bright, curious people that &quot;want to experience the weird.&quot; They are people that very much unlike 20-30 years ago, we're not steeped in the Church and want to experience the esoteric aspects of the Christian Faith. This was very striking for me, since it sounded downright occult in the way he described it (although probably only to my ear). It made me wonder what would happen if those types of people got Embraced into the Lancea et Sanctum. It also made me wonder if those people wouldn't try and blend the Lance and the Circle. <br /> <br /> Here's why: what he described was young theologians who weren't afraid to experiment or put themselves in other people shoes. If there was some odd or quirky (my words, not his) new practice, they don't want to hear about it, they want to be right in there participating. If someone like that gets Embraced and their powerful faith stays intact, I have no doubt that they'd fly straight into the arms of the Shadow Church, but I also have little doubt that they'd also continue on experimenting and eventually find themselves at least overlapping with the outskirts of the Circle of the Crone. <br /> <br /> Thoughts? Suggestions? Are there idea worth pursuing in here, or am I out to lunch?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://tinyurl.com/2p8ss75f\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Chris H</b></a>/<b>EndDaysEngine</b> | <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/EndDaysEngine\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Patreon</b></a></span>| <b>He/His </b>| <b>Currently Writing</b>: <i>[Redacted], [Redacted]</i><br /> <br /> <b>CofD booklists: </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/DRdB7h\" target=\"_blank\">Beast</a> I <a href=\"https://goo.gl/x1FPpM\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/ZSCx5U\" target=\"_blank\">Demon</a> | <a href=\"http://shorturl.at/syDK9\" target=\"_blank\">Deviant</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/Mh5M6m\" target=\"_blank\">Geist</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/gSjfm5\" target=\"_blank\">Hunter</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/qj5aX9\" target=\"_blank\">Mage</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/2HCpjg\" target=\"_blank\">Mummy</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/68yRHY\" target=\"_blank\">Promethean</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/a4mjqA\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/hrULtN\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4433-second-chances","name":"Second Chances","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-10T01:42:48-08:00","id":805082,"title":"","content":"It's a legitimate idea. Religions change, people change, interest rates fluctuate\u2014so it makes sense that someone who's raised in a less hidebound religion tradition (or even starting out non religious themselves) would attempt this path as as member of the Lancea et Sanctum.<br /> <br /> Of course, it also depends on how open-minded the rest of the Kindred that make up the local aspect of that covenant are. Elder members of the Lance might be a bit more hardlined. Younger members less so (unless they're rabidly trying to please their elders). Whomever was running the local church would also have a determination in what could be considered canon, acceptable, or heretical.<br /> <br /> Building a better monster is something that isn't just behind the Lance; it's part of the Ordo Dracul as well. Only problem is the Dragons seek to essentially spit in God's eye and transcend their condition for their own sake, not to please God as God's wolves preying upon the flock of humanity. On the other side of the spectrum, yes, the Circle have some useful ideas about the spirit (and spirits, and entities, and demiurges) and God (and/or God(s)), so that's another area some younger Kindred might also feel inclined to explore. (Again, though, if a Hardliner elder Lance member gets wind of it, especially if it's proscribed, even God may not be able to help said young blood.)<br /> <br /> I'm also wondering, as covenants, kindred, and human society all continue to evolve, what could come of someone attempting to syncretize what's learned of all three of these Covenants.<br /> <br /> (I really should re-read the Lancea covenant book soon; I remember there being all manner of factions within the church itself; not merely a monolithic entity.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#000080\">&quot;There is the world of light, and the world of darkness. And some in the light prefer the darkness.&quot; \u2014 Loren Eiseley, <i>The Night Country</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/583-zsander","name":"Zsander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar583_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"170"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-10T23:47:47-08:00","id":805846,"title":"","content":"I get the impression from reading the <i>Testament of Longinus</i> that the Lancea et Sanctum believe that the Damned have been molded by God to suit the purpose he's set out for them. This includes the weaknesses of the vampiric condition.The Damned are <i>meant </i>to fear the sun and the burning brand, and they are <i>meant</i> to grapple with their hunger and their rage. To circumvent these weaknesses is to circumvent the will of the Lord, which is the very essence of the sin of Pride. Hence why the L+S has a big problem with the Ordo Dracul.<br /> <br /> That said, I think that many if not most of the Sanctified would heartily endorse the idea of becoming a better monster in service to the Lord. They simply have a different idea about what constitutes a &quot;better monster&quot; than the Order. Improving your Disciplines is certainly alright, as they are gifts inherent to one's Damnation. Studying and refining the rituals of Theban Sorcery is appropriate as well, as those arts were revealed to the faithful by an angel of the Lord and continue to be developed through divine inspiration.<br /> <br /> Perhaps more importantly, the Damned can seek to understand God's will more completely, through prayer, studying holy texts and the writings of past leaders of the Chapel, meditating on holy relics, and conferring with local religious authorities. Any of the Damned my serve God's purpose in a general sense by simply observing the Traditions and fulfilling their need to feed, but surely they are capable of accomplishing far more when their Requiems are properly guided.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3209,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","user":"CatDoom","date":". 01-11-2016, 02:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-11T05:45:53-08:00","id":806009,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CatDoom</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/804694-lancea-et-sanctum-and-theology?p=805846#post805846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I get the impression from reading the <i>Testament of Longinus</i> that the Lancea et Sanctum believe that the Damned have been molded by God to suit the purpose he's set out for them. This includes the weaknesses of the vampiric condition.The Damned are <i>meant </i>to fear the sun and the burning brand, and they are <i>meant</i> to grapple with their hunger and their rage. To circumvent these weaknesses is to circumvent the will of the Lord, which is the very essence of the sin of Pride. Hence why the L+S has a big problem with the Ordo Dracul.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is all subject to interpretation. Yes, God created vampires with certain weaknesses, but mortals can and do have numerous shortcomings in their nature. Most Christian denominations would argue that even if you are more short-tempered, vengeful or lazy than others, it does not entitle you to just ignore these parts of your own self and cease trying to master them, rather than allowing them to master you. &quot;I am just born this way&quot; is not a valid argument in the world of Christian theology. <br /> <br /> By that logic, it'd be reasonable for L+S members to try to overcome their &quot;inherent&quot; weaknesses and condemn accepting them as stagnation, and besides: if the Spear teaches that vampires have immortal souls (do they? I honestly don't remember), it means they can be redeemed too, and there is no better way to do that than reclaiming parts of your human experience. All this would probably be far from mainstream teachings, but enough young and open-minded Damned might do the trick.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10396-woland","name":"Woland","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10396_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-11T08:21:39-08:00","id":806096,"title":"","content":"Honestly, you're right; interpretations of the <i>Testament </i>probably vary considerably from domain to domain. As for the possibility of the Damned being redeemable, that seems to be a definite point of controversy within the Sanctified ranks. The most conservative answer seems to be that it <i>may</i> be possible, but that the Damned will remain so at least until Judgement Day, and that until then it's their responsibility to behave as monsters to serve the purpose God has set for them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-11T08:54:41-08:00","id":806128,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Second Chances</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/804694-lancea-et-sanctum-and-theology#post804694\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">These too covenants usually stand pretty far apart, since the Lancea sees the vampiric condition as being divinely bestowed (in a damning way, yes, but still divine), while the Ordo wants to transcend that very same condition. The conflict is obvious, but does it have to be inevitable? In most cases they will clash, but I think I see a way that they could synthesize their beliefs together, if they are working with the definition of a theologian given above.</div> </div> </div> </div> I think they generally clash because people tend to play up the whole &quot;religious zealtry&quot; angle of the LeS. Theologans want to preserve any scripture or information. The LeS actively destroy anything that disagrees with them. Histories are rewritten, detrimental information is burned, and heresies are buried.<br /> <br /> That's the problem with the Lance. Far too often, they're portrayed in the worst possible light, as blind fantatics who are actively antagonistic to free thinking. Not even in just some books, but in play. Its like the intersection of science and religion. A number of people actively think that the two must be mutually opposed. No, they don't have to be. Especially not when, in the CoD, there is ample evidence of higher powers at work. Most notably, science can objectively prove that God(-Machine) exists, as do Its angels. It really puts a new spin on atheism and religion when its all -real-. God, the Dark Mother, Enlightenment. <br /> <br /> Anyways, one of the most interesting things about the Ordo Dracul is that they're the Wild Card. The Invictus-Carthians are a political axis. The Lancea-Circle are a religious axis. They balance each other out. The Strix come in and wreck everything to chaos. The Ordo Dracul? They play the balance whatever side they want. This means that they play up all four of the other covenants to get what they want.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The other big idea that I pulled out for Vampire (although it really applies to all of CofD) cam from the question period at the end. Someone asked what commonality the lecturer saw in the students of theology that he teaches, since it is neither as prestigious as it used to be, nor is it a profession that can be more the part-time. His answer was super interesting. All of them seem to be bright, curious people that &quot;want to experience the weird.&quot; They are people that very much unlike 20-30 years ago, we're not steeped in the Church and want to experience the esoteric aspects of the Christian Faith. This was very striking for me, since it sounded downright occult in the way he described it (although probably only to my ear). It made me wonder what would happen if those types of people got Embraced into the Lancea et Sanctum. It also made me wonder if those people wouldn't try and blend the Lance and the Circle. </div> </div> </div> This is actually a movement within the church as a whole. The esoteric tends to get an occult label, which is only fair, since technically they're kind of similar. Esoteric material tends to be more difficult to find, and the occult literally means &quot;the hidden.&quot; <br /> <br /> Either way, what I'm getting around to eventually is that this attitude isn't actually unheard of in the Chruch. In the past, monks were the source of acurate history writing, science, experiementation and rising. Eventually, the Church became calcified, stagnant. Then there's an Awakening, people become more interested in exploring new ideas again. Its a cycle that's quite common in the history of the world.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Thoughts? Suggestions? Are there idea worth pursuing in here, or am I out to lunch? </div> </div> </div> Its a generation thing. Elder vampires who've been around for a while are difficult to change, while the newly Emrbaced (and those freshly risen from torpor!) are more adventurous. Its totally worth exploring... if anyone is interested in playing a Lance game. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}